# DAP session — an exception stop names the exception's TYPE. The
# `stopped` event's `text` is the type (DAP: "the exception name") and
# `description` the message — debugpy: `text: "ZeroDivisionError"`,
# `description: "division by zero"`; the fake: `Error` with the runtime
# error's message. The toast and the console note say `type: message`.
# A client that showed `description` alone said "stopped (division by
# zero)" and never the class, which is the first thing anyone reads
# (hunt: dap-exception-stop-drops-type). The program text deliberately
# does not contain the word `Error`, so only the stop can put it on
# screen.
write prog.dbg "let x = 1\nprint nope\nprint x\n"
write .mnml/config.zon .{ .dap = .{ .dbg = .{ .cmd = "$MNML_FAKE_DAP" } } }
open prog.dbg
command editor.use_vim

command dap.run
expect screen contains "dap: stopped (Error: cannot print `nope`: no such variable)"
expect screen contains "▶"
command dap.show
expect pane "Debug"
# (the note is clipped by the pane's width; the toast above carried it whole)
expect screen contains "exception — Error: cannot print `nope`"
expect screen contains "prog.dbg:2  main"

command dap.terminate
expect screen contains "dap: terminated"
